hadiths[3]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2696","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb, (who said): My paternal uncle narrated to me, (who said): Abu Sakhr narrated to me, from Ibn Qusayt, from ‘Ubayd ibn Hunayn, who said:\nI performed Hajj with ‘Abd Allah ibn ‘Umar ibn al-Khattab twelve times, between Hajj and ‘Umrah.\nHe said: I said to him: “O Abu ‘Abd al-Rahman, I have seen from you four characteristics,” and he mentioned the hadith, and said: “I saw you when you entered the state of Ihram, upon entering the *‘arsh* (the area surrounding the Ka‘bah), you would stop reciting the Talbiyah.”\nHe said: “You have spoken the truth, O Ibn Hunayn. I went out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and when he entered the *‘arsh*, he stopped reciting the Talbiyah. So my Talbiyah will not cease until I die.”\n\nAbu Bakr said: “I used to think that the Talbiyah of the one performing ‘Umrah should continue until he touches the Black Stone at the beginning of his Tawaf for his ‘Umrah, based on the report of Ibn Abi Layla, from ‘Ata’, from Ibn ‘Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to stop reciting the Talbiyah for ‘Umrah upon touching the Black Stone.”"
"2697","**\nNarrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi and Muhammad ibn Hisham, who said: Narrated to us Hushaym, who informed me Ibn Abi Layla, who said Muhammad ibn Hisham narrated from Ibn Abi Layla, who said Abu Bakr (RA) said:\n\nWhen I reflected upon the report of 'Ubayd ibn Hunayn, it contained evidence that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to stop reciting the talbiyah upon entering the *ʿurūsh* of Makkah. The report of 'Ubayd ibn Hunayn is more firmly established in its chain than the report of 'Aṭāʾ, because Ibn Abi Layla was not meticulous in memorization, even though he was a jurist and scholar.\n\nI hold the view that a person in ihram performing Hajj, or 'Umrah, or both, should stop reciting the talbiyah upon entering the *ʿurūsh* of Makkah. If he is performing 'Umrah, he should not resume the talbiyah. If he is performing Hajj (Ifrad) or Qirān, he should resume the talbiyah after completing the saʿy between al-Safā and al-Marwah, because the practice of Ibn ʿUmar (RA) indicates that he saw the Prophet (ﷺ) stop the talbiyah during his Hajj until he completed the saʿy between al-Safā and al-Marwah.\n\nNarrated to us al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān, who narrated to us Bashshār ibn Bakr, from al-Awzāʿī, who said ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ (RA) said:\n\nIbn ʿUmar (RA) used to stop reciting the talbiyah upon entering the *ḥaram*, and he would resume it after completing his tawāf between al-Safā and al-Marwah."
"2698","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Mahdi al-'Attar, who said: Narrated to us 'Amr—meaning Ibn Abi Salamah—who said: Narrated to me Ibn Zubr—he is 'Abdullah ibn al-'Ala' ibn Zubr—who said: Narrated to me al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, who said: I saw 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar (RA) cease reciting the Talbiyah upon entering the Haram (sacred precincts of Makkah), then resume it after completing the Tawaf (circumambulation) of the House, and after finishing the Tawaf between Safa and Marwah.\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: The reports from the Prophet (ﷺ) indicate that he (ﷺ) continued reciting the Talbiyah until he stoned the Jamrat al-'Aqabah. This proves that he (ﷺ) did not cease the Talbiyah upon entering the Haram, nor did he resume it after a pause...\n\nI will mention his Talbiyah until he stoned the Jamrat al-'Aqabah in its proper place in this book, if Allah wills and grants success."
